{{para}}. The Plaintiff claims against the Defendant:

(a) damages for wrongful dismissal and breach of the employment contract in the sum of ${{total_claim}}, representing {{common_law_high}} months' pay in lieu of reasonable notice, or such other amount as this Honourable Court deems just;

(b) the sum equal to the value of all employment-related benefits and perquisites throughout the {{common_law_high}}-month notice period, including without limitation health benefits, dental coverage, life insurance, RRSP contributions, car allowance, cell phone allowance, and any other benefits or perquisites forming part of the Plaintiff's total compensation;

{{#if has_bonus}}
(c) damages representing the value of all bonus compensation the Plaintiff would have received during the notice period;
{{/if}}

{{#if has_equity_comp}}
(d) damages representing the value of all equity-based compensation, including stock options, restricted share units, and performance share units, that would have vested or been exercisable during the notice period;
{{/if}}

{{#if unpaid_commission}}
(e) damages representing the value of all commissions and variable compensation earned but unpaid at termination, and that would have accrued during the notice period;
{{/if}}

(f) damages equal to termination pay and severance pay pursuant to the Employment Standards Act, 2000, S.O. 2000, c. 41 (the "ESA"), to the extent not already paid, without deduction or regard to mitigation;

(g) damages for unpaid vacation pay and statutory holiday pay pursuant to the ESA;

{{#if unpaid_overtime}}
(h) damages for unpaid overtime wages pursuant to the ESA;
{{/if}}

{{#if expenses_unreimbursed}}
(i) reimbursement of all business expenses incurred by the Plaintiff and remaining unreimbursed at termination;
{{/if}}

{{#if bad_faith_acts}}
(i) punitive, aggravated, Bhasin, and/or moral damages in the amount of ${{aggravated_damages}}, or such other amount as this Honourable Court deems just;
{{/if}}

{{#if gate_G10_fired}}
(j) damages for violations of the Human Rights Code, R.S.O. 1990, c. H.19 (the "Code"), for injury to dignity, feelings, and self-respect;
{{/if}}

(k) special damages equal to the costs incurred by the Plaintiff in seeking to obtain comparable employment, in an amount to be proven at trial;

(l) pre-judgment and post-judgment interest pursuant to the Courts of Justice Act, R.S.O. 1990, c. C.43, as amended;

(m) the costs and disbursements of this action on a substantial indemnity basis together with applicable taxes; and

(n) such further and other relief as this Honourable Court deems just.

{{para}}. The Plaintiff commenced employment with the Defendant on or about {{hire_date}}{{#if has_written_contract}} pursuant to a written employment agreement dated {{contract_date}}{{/if}}.

{{para}}. Most recently, the Plaintiff held the position of {{position_title}}. In this role, the Plaintiff was responsible for {{duties_description}}.

{{#if positive_performance}}
{{para}}. Throughout {{pronoun_possessive}} employment, the Plaintiff was a dedicated and committed employee who received positive performance reviews and recognition throughout {{pronoun_possessive}} tenure with the Defendant.
{{else}}
{{para}}. Throughout {{pronoun_possessive}} employment, the Plaintiff was a dedicated and committed employee.
{{/if}}

{{para}}. At the time of termination, the Plaintiff's total compensation was comprised of the following:

- Approximately {{salary}} per year in base salary;
{{#if has_bonus}}- Bonus compensation of approximately {{bonus_amount}} per year;{{/if}}
{{#if has_equity_comp}}- Equity-based compensation including {{equity_type}};{{/if}}
{{#if unpaid_commission}}- Commission and variable compensation of approximately {{commission_amount}} per year;{{/if}}
{{#if has_car_allowance}}- A car allowance of approximately {{car_allowance}} per month;{{/if}}
{{#if has_benefits}}- Health, dental, life insurance, and extended benefits;{{/if}}
{{#if has_rrsp}}- RRSP matching contributions of {{rrsp_amount}} per year;{{/if}}
{{#if other_compensation}}- Additional compensation and perquisites including {{other_compensation_list}}.{{/if}}

{{para}}. On or about {{termination_date}}, the Defendant terminated the Plaintiff's employment without cause. The Plaintiff was {{years_of_service}} years into {{pronoun_possessive}} employment at the time of termination.

{{para}}. The Defendant failed to provide the Plaintiff with reasonable notice of termination or pay in lieu thereof at common law. Any amount provided by the Defendant upon termination was inadequate and does not represent the Plaintiff's entitlement.

{{#if esa_shortfall}}
{{para}}. The Defendant further failed to provide the Plaintiff with notice of termination, pay in lieu thereof, or severance pay as required by the Employment Standards Act, 2000, S.O. 2000, c. 41, or provided amounts less than the statutory minimums to which the Plaintiff was entitled.
{{/if}}

{{#if bad_faith_termination_manner}}
{{para}}. The Plaintiff further pleads that the manner in which {{pronoun_possessive}} employment was terminated was callous, insensitive, and conducted in bad faith. Specifically, {{bad_faith_termination_particulars}}. The Defendant failed to act in a candid, reasonable, honest, and forthright manner in the course of the Plaintiff's dismissal.
{{/if}}

{{para}}. The Plaintiff's employment agreement contains a provision purporting to limit {{pronoun_possessive}} entitlement to notice upon termination (the "Termination Clause"). The relevant portion of the agreement reads as follows:

{{#if term_clause_text}}<blockquote>{{term_clause_text}}</blockquote>{{/if}}

{{para}}. The Plaintiff pleads that the Termination Clause is void and unenforceable on the following grounds, each of which is independent and sufficient:

{{#if clause_cause_broader}}
(a) the for-cause provision employs a standard broader than the wilful misconduct, disobedience, or wilful neglect of duty threshold required by Ontario Regulation 288/01 under the Employment Standards Act, 2000, S.O. 2000, c. 41, rendering the Termination Clause void in its entirety;
{{/if}}

{{#if clause_no_benefits}}
(b) the Termination Clause fails to provide for the continuation of the Plaintiff's group benefits during the statutory notice period as required by section 60(1)(c) of the Employment Standards Act, 2000, S.O. 2000, c. 41;
{{/if}}

{{#if clause_limits_below_esa}}
(c) the Termination Clause could operate, at the time it was entered into or at any point during the employment, to provide the Plaintiff with less than the minimum entitlements prescribed by the Employment Standards Act, 2000, S.O. 2000, c. 41, contrary to section 5(1) of that Act;
{{/if}}

(d) the Termination Clause is ambiguous, vague, and does not clearly and unambiguously displace the Plaintiff's entitlement to reasonable notice at common law; and

(e) the Termination Clause is unconscionable in that it was presented to the Plaintiff on a take-it-or-leave-it basis, the Plaintiff did not receive independent legal advice, and there was a significant imbalance of bargaining power between the parties at the time the agreement was signed.

{{#if employer_breached_clause}}
(f) the Defendant breached the terms of the Termination Clause, thereby repudiating it, rendering it of no force and effect.
{{/if}}

{{para}}. In the absence of an enforceable provision limiting the Plaintiff's notice entitlement to the applicable statutory minimums, the Termination Clause fails to displace the common law. The Plaintiff is entitled to reasonable notice of termination at common law.

{{para}}. In the alternative, any ambiguity in the Termination Clause is to be construed against the Defendant as the drafter, in accordance with the principle of contra proferentem. The interpretation least favourable to the Defendant is that the Plaintiff is entitled to reasonable notice at common law.

{{para}}. In the absence of an enforceable termination provision limiting the Plaintiff's entitlement to notice, the Plaintiff is entitled to reasonable notice of termination at common law.

{{para}}. The appropriate period of reasonable notice is assessed having regard to all relevant circumstances, including the following factors as they apply to the Plaintiff:

- Age: The Plaintiff was {{age}} years of age at the time of termination;
- Length of service: The Plaintiff served the Defendant for {{years_of_service}} years;
- Character of employment: The Plaintiff held the position of {{position_title}}, {{job_level_description}}; 
- Availability of comparable employment: The market for comparable positions is limited, and the Plaintiff's specialized skills, experience, and qualifications narrow the field of available opportunities; and
- Such further and other factors as may be relevant to the assessment of reasonable notice in the circumstances of this case.

{{#if prior_related_employer}}
{{para}}. In addition, the Plaintiff pleads that {{pronoun_possessive}} prior service of {{prior_service}} years with {{predecessor}} is a factor to be taken into account in assessing the appropriate notice period, as the Defendant received the full benefit of the experience and institutional knowledge the Plaintiff developed during that prior service.
{{/if}}

{{#if employer_initiated_recruitment}}
{{para}}. Further, the Plaintiff pleads that {{pronoun_subject}} was actively recruited by the Defendant from secure employment with {{prior_employer}}, where {{pronoun_subject}} had been employed for {{prior_service}} years. The inducement from secure long-term employment is an independent factor supporting a longer notice period.
{{/if}}

{{para}}. Having regard to all of the foregoing factors, the Plaintiff pleads that {{pronoun_subject}} is entitled to reasonable notice of {{common_law_high}} months, or pay in lieu thereof in the amount of ${{total_claim}}, or such other period and amount as this Honourable Court deems appropriate.

{{para}}. The Plaintiff's damages in lieu of reasonable notice are calculated on the basis of {{pronoun_possessive}} total compensation package for the duration of the notice period, not base salary alone, including all components of compensation set out above.

{{para}}. The Plaintiff pleads that the Defendant breached its duty of good faith and fair dealing in the manner of {{pronoun_possessive}} dismissal. Specifically, the Defendant: {{bad_faith_acts_list}}.

{{para}}. The Defendant's conduct was untruthful, misleading, and unduly insensitive, and constituted a failure to act in a candid, reasonable, honest, and forthright manner toward the Plaintiff in the course of {{pronoun_possessive}} dismissal.

{{#if mental_distress_symptoms}}
{{para}}. The Defendant's conduct caused the Plaintiff mental distress beyond the ordinary upset associated with any termination of employment, including {{mental_distress_symptoms}}.
{{/if}}

{{#if false_cause_alleged}}
{{para}}. The Defendant advanced an allegation of cause against the Plaintiff at the time of termination, specifically {{cause_reasons}}. The Defendant knew or ought to have known that those grounds were unsupportable. The advancement of a false cause allegation is itself an act of bad faith in the manner of dismissal and an independent actionable wrong.
{{/if}}

{{para}}. The Defendant's conduct constitutes an independent actionable wrong. The Plaintiff accordingly claims aggravated, punitive, Bhasin, and/or moral damages against the Defendant, in an amount to be determined by this Honourable Court.

{{para}}. The Plaintiff pleads that the Defendant failed to satisfy its statutory obligations to the Plaintiff under the Employment Standards Act, 2000, S.O. 2000, c. 41 (the "ESA").

{{#if esa_term_shortfall}}
{{para}}. Pursuant to section 57 of the ESA, the Plaintiff was entitled to {{esa_notice_weeks}} weeks' termination pay upon the termination of {{pronoun_possessive}} employment. The Defendant failed to provide this amount in full. The Plaintiff claims the outstanding balance as a debt owing independently of any common law entitlement.
{{/if}}

{{#if esa_sev_shortfall}}
{{para}}. Pursuant to section 64 of the ESA, the Plaintiff was entitled to severance pay calculated on the basis of {{pronoun_possessive}} {{years_of_service}} completed years of service. The Defendant failed to provide the correct amount. The Plaintiff claims the outstanding balance as a debt owing independently of any common law entitlement.
{{/if}}

{{#if vacation_unpaid}}
{{para}}. Pursuant to the Employment Standards Act, 2000, S.O. 2000, c. 41, the Plaintiff was entitled to vacation pay on all wages earned throughout the employment, calculated in accordance with the applicable provisions of the ESA. The Defendant failed to pay out all accrued and unused vacation pay upon termination. The Plaintiff claims all outstanding vacation pay as a debt owing.
{{/if}}

{{#if vacation_underpaid_rate}}
{{para}}. Pursuant to section 35.2 of the ESA, the Defendant was required to pay the Plaintiff vacation pay of not less than four percent of all wages earned, rising to six percent upon the Plaintiff completing five years of employment. Throughout the employment, the Defendant paid vacation pay at a rate below the statutory minimum. The Plaintiff claims all vacation pay underpaid throughout the employment as a debt owing.
{{/if}}

{{#if vacation_excluded_variable_comp}}
{{para}}. "Wages" as defined in section 1 of the ESA includes commissions and non-discretionary bonus payments. The Defendant calculated the Plaintiff's vacation pay on base salary alone and excluded the Plaintiff's commissions and bonus payments from that calculation. The Plaintiff claims all vacation pay owing on such wages throughout the employment as a debt owing.
{{/if}}

{{#if holiday_pay_unpaid}}
{{para}}. Pursuant to Part X of the ESA, the Plaintiff was entitled to public holiday pay calculated in accordance with section 24 of the ESA. The Defendant failed to pay the public holiday pay to which the Plaintiff was entitled. The Plaintiff claims all unpaid public holiday pay as a debt owing.
{{/if}}

{{#if unpaid_commission}}
{{para}}. As at the date of termination, the Plaintiff had earned commissions and bonus amounts that remained unpaid. Such amounts are wages within the meaning of section 1 of the ESA and were required to be paid in accordance with section 11(5) of the ESA. The Plaintiff claims all earned and unpaid commissions and bonuses as a debt owing.
{{/if}}

{{#if expenses_unreimbursed}}
{{para}}. The Plaintiff incurred expenses in the course of {{pronoun_possessive}} duties that the Defendant was obligated to reimburse. As at the date of termination, such expenses remained unreimbursed. The Plaintiff claims all unreimbursed expenses as a debt owing.
{{/if}}

{{#if unpaid_overtime}}
{{para}}. The Plaintiff pleads that despite {{pronoun_possessive}} title, {{pronoun_possessive}} duties were primarily non-managerial in nature. The Plaintiff regularly performed {{overtime_duties_description}} and was entitled to overtime pay at one and one-half times {{pronoun_possessive}} regular rate for all hours worked in excess of 44 hours per week pursuant to section 22 of the ESA. The Defendant failed to pay the required overtime. The Plaintiff claims all unpaid overtime as a debt owing.
{{/if}}

{{#if benefits_not_continued}}
{{para}}. Pursuant to section 60(1)(c) of the ESA, the Defendant was required to continue all employee benefits throughout the statutory notice period. The Defendant failed to do so. The Plaintiff claims the value of all discontinued benefits for the duration of the statutory notice period as damages.
{{/if}}

{{#if unauthorized_deductions}}
{{para}}. The Defendant made unauthorized deductions from the Plaintiff's wages contrary to section 13(1) of the ESA. No lawful basis existed for those deductions. The Plaintiff claims repayment of all amounts improperly deducted.
{{/if}}

{{para}}. The Defendant's failure to satisfy its statutory obligations constitutes an independent breach of the ESA and an actionable wrong giving rise to damages separate from and in addition to the Plaintiff's common law entitlements.

{{para}}. The Plaintiff pleads that the Defendant discriminated against {{pronoun_object}} in {{pronoun_possessive}} employment on the basis of {{protected_ground}}, contrary to section 5(1) of the Human Rights Code, R.S.O. 1990, c. H.19 (the "Code").

{{para}}. The Plaintiff was subjected to the following discriminatory conduct in the workplace: {{hrc_conduct_description}}. The discriminatory conduct was connected to the Plaintiff's {{protected_ground}} and created a poisoned work environment.

{{#if hrc_complaint_made}}
{{para}}. On or about {{hrc_complaint_date}}, the Plaintiff raised {{pronoun_possessive}} concerns regarding the discriminatory treatment with {{hrc_complaint_recipient}}. The Defendant failed to adequately investigate or address the Plaintiff's complaint.
{{/if}}

{{para}}. The Plaintiff's termination followed {{hrc_termination_timing}} {{pronoun_possessive}} protected activity and discriminatory treatment. The Plaintiff pleads that {{pronoun_possessive}} {{protected_ground}} was a factor in the decision to terminate {{pronoun_possessive}} employment.

{{#if hrc_complaint_made}}
{{para}}. Further or in the alternative, the Plaintiff pleads that the termination was connected to {{pronoun_possessive}} decision to assert {{pronoun_possessive}} rights under the Code, and constitutes retaliation for same.
{{/if}}

{{#if accommodation_requested}}
{{para}}. The Plaintiff requested accommodation of {{pronoun_possessive}} {{accommodation_ground}}. The Defendant failed to accommodate the Plaintiff to the point of undue hardship as required by section 17 of the Code. The Defendant did not conduct a meaningful accommodation inquiry or implement any workable solution.
{{/if}}

{{#if sexual_harassment}}
{{para}}. The Plaintiff was subjected to unwanted sexual comments, conduct, and solicitation in the workplace contrary to section 7 of the Code. The Defendant failed to take reasonable steps to prevent or address the conduct. The perpetrator was {{harasser_relationship}} to the Plaintiff.
{{/if}}

{{para}}. As a result of the Defendant's conduct, the Plaintiff suffered injury to {{pronoun_possessive}} dignity, feelings, and self-respect. The Plaintiff claims damages pursuant to the Code in an amount to be determined by this Honourable Court.

{{#if cd_primary}}
{{para}}. The Plaintiff pleads that {{pronoun_subject}} was constructively dismissed. The Defendant unilaterally imposed the following changes to the Plaintiff's employment without {{pronoun_possessive}} consent: {{cd_changes}}. These changes constituted a breach of an essential term of the employment relationship.
{{/if}}

{{#if cd_alternative}}
{{para}}. In the further alternative, the Plaintiff pleads that prior to the formal termination of {{pronoun_possessive}} employment, the Defendant unilaterally imposed the following changes without the Plaintiff's consent: {{cd_changes}}. These changes constituted a breach of an essential term of the employment relationship and would have entitled the Plaintiff to treat the employment as terminated independently of the formal termination.
{{/if}}

{{para}}. Assessed objectively, a reasonable person in the Plaintiff's position would have concluded that the Defendant no longer intended to be bound by the terms of the employment relationship.

{{#if cd_cumulative}}
{{para}}. In the further alternative, the Plaintiff pleads that the Defendant's cumulative course of conduct, taken as a whole, demonstrated that it no longer intended to be bound by the terms of the employment relationship. The pattern of conduct amounted to a repudiation of the employment contract.
{{/if}}

{{#if cd_primary}}
{{para}}. The Plaintiff did not condone the changes. {{pronoun_subject}} elected to treat the employment as terminated on {{termination_date}} and did so within a reasonable time of the Defendant's breach. The constructive dismissal constitutes a wrongful dismissal and the Plaintiff is entitled to damages in lieu of reasonable notice on the same basis as if {{pronoun_subject}} had been formally terminated without cause.
{{/if}}

{{#if cd_remote}}
{{para}}. Among the unilateral changes imposed by the Defendant was a requirement that the Plaintiff attend {{workplace_location}} on {{attendance_frequency}}, contrary to the Plaintiff's established remote work arrangement. The Plaintiff's employment agreement expressly provided, or the parties had a settled mutual understanding, that the Plaintiff would work remotely from {{remote_work_location}}. The Defendant had no contractual basis to unilaterally alter this arrangement.
{{/if}}

{{para}}. The Plaintiff pleads that at all material times the Defendants operated as common employers and related employers with respect to the Plaintiff's employment.

{{para}}. The Defendants' operations were interrelated and integrated, including but not limited to:

(a) carrying on business from a common head office or shared premises;
{{#if shared_branding}}(b) operating under shared branding, business names, or a unified enterprise identity;{{/if}}
{{#if shared_management}}(c) sharing management personnel, including senior leadership, human resources, and on-site supervision;{{/if}}
{{#if shared_payroll}}(d) centralizing human resources, payroll, and administrative functions; and{{/if}}
(e) jointly directing and controlling the terms and conditions of the Plaintiff's employment, including hiring, supervision, discipline, and termination.

{{para}}. The Plaintiff's employment documentation reflected the integrated nature of the Defendants' operations, including: {{common_employer_documentation}}.

{{para}}. Each of the Defendants exercised control and direction over the Plaintiff's employment. Each Defendant intended to and did create an employment relationship with the Plaintiff.

{{para}}. The Defendants are jointly and severally liable to the Plaintiff for all amounts claimed in this proceeding as common employers at common law and pursuant to applicable employment and human rights legislation.

{{#if breach_express_term}}
{{para}}. The Plaintiff pleads that the Defendant breached the following express term or terms of the employment agreement: {{express_term_description}}. The Defendant was contractually obligated to {{express_term_obligation}} and failed to do so.
{{/if}}

{{#if breach_implied_term}}
{{para}}. The Plaintiff {{#if breach_express_term}}further {{/if}}pleads that the Defendant breached terms implied by law into the employment agreement. Specifically, the Defendant's conduct, including {{implied_term_conduct}}, constituted a breach of the implied term that the Defendant would not act in a manner calculated or likely to destroy or seriously damage the relationship of trust and confidence between employer and employee.
{{/if}}

{{para}}. As a result of the Defendant's breach, the Plaintiff suffered loss and damage. The Plaintiff claims damages for breach of contract in an amount to be determined by this Honourable Court, including all amounts owing under the express or implied terms breached, and consequential losses flowing from the breach.
